---

<GuidePage title='Error Boundaries - App Router Guide' root='..'>
  <div class='kicker'>Chapter 12 · Part B: Contain the Unknown</div>
  <h1>Error Boundaries</h1>
  <p class='lede'>
    Failure is not a component concern either; it is structural, the exact twin of loading. An error
    boundary moves "what shows when a subtree throws" out of the component and into the tree, so one
    failure degrades one region instead of blanking the page. This chapter is <code>error.tsx</code>
    and its granularity, isolating regions with parallel routes, and dropping to custom boundaries when
    the file convention cannot reach far enough.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href='11-suspense-streaming.html'>§11</a> made the case that loading belongs to the tree, not the
    component: a <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code> boundary above a component decides what shows while it
    waits, and <code>loading.tsx</code> is that boundary drawn around a route segment. Errors are the
    same story with a different verb. A component that throws should not decide what the user sees any
    more than a component that suspends should; a boundary above it should. The App Router gives you the
    same two tools it gave you for loading: a file convention (<code>error.tsx</code>) for the route
    segment, and a plain React boundary you place by hand for anything finer.
  </p>

  <h2 id='mechanism'>12.1 The boundary catches the throw</h2>
  <p>
    An <code>error.tsx</code> file is a <strong
      >Client Component that Next.js wraps as a React error boundary around the segment</strong
    >: the page and everything rendered beneath it.
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='critical' /> When anything in that subtree throws while rendering, the boundary swaps the
    subtree for the file's fallback and hands it two things: the <code>error</code> (with a
    <code>digest</code> that ties the client fallback to the server-side stack trace in your logs) and
    <code>reset()</code>, a function that re-renders the segment to retry. The symmetry with
    <a href='11-suspense-streaming.html#mechanism'>§11.1</a> is exact:
    <code>loading.tsx</code> is to <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code> what
    <code>error.tsx</code> is to the error boundary. Next.js wraps every segment as roughly
    <code
      >&lt;ErrorBoundary&gt;&lt;Suspense&gt;{'{page}'}&lt;/Suspense&gt;&lt;/ErrorBoundary&gt;</code
    >: the layout persists, Suspense contains the waiting, the boundary contains the failing.
  </p>

