<span class="tok-kw">import</span> { useEffect } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"react"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export default function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramError</span>({
  error,
  reset,
}: {
  error: <span class="tok-fn">Error</span> &amp; { digest?: <span class="tok-kw">string</span> };
  reset: () =&gt; <span class="tok-kw">void</span>;
}) {
  <span class="tok-fn">useEffect</span>(() =&gt; {
    <span class="tok-com">/* Log to your error service; the digest ties this client
       fallback to the server-side stack in the logs. */</span>
    <span class="tok-fn">reportError</span>(error);
  }, [error]);

  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ErrorState</span> <span class="tok-prop">title</span>=<span class="tok-str">"This program failed to load"</span>&gt;
      &lt;button <span class="tok-prop">onClick</span>={reset}&gt;Try again&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">ErrorState</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      Next wraps the segment (the page and everything under it) in this boundary. A throw anywhere
      in that subtree swaps it for this fallback; <code>reset()</code> re-renders the segment to retry.
      Like every boundary, it is a client component, because catching a render error and holding fallback
      state is inherently stateful client work.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <h2 id='container'>12.2 The fallback slots into a reserved container</h2>
  <p>
    An error fallback is the definition of unknown content: you cannot know in advance whether it
    will render, or when. So it obeys the rule every unknown obeys (<a
      href='10-containers-content-shifting.html'>§10</a
    >): the container is static and known, it reserves the space, and the fallback slots into it.
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='very' /> The boundary does not replace the container or collapse the layout around it; it
    swaps what fills a box the layout already drew and sized. Keep the fallback itself plain, an image
    and a message centered in the reserved space, so a failure reads as "this region could not load" and
    not as a hole where a region used to be.
  </p>

