  <p>
    This is why loading, empty, and error are three fills of one box (<a
      href='11-suspense-streaming.html#orthogonal'>§11.6</a
    >): each is unknown content dropped into the same reserved container, centered the same way,
    leaving the same footprint. Size the container once, in the layout or scaffold (<a
      href='10-containers-content-shifting.html#layouts'>§10.4</a
    >), and every one of those states inherits a stable place to live.
  </p>

  <h2 id='granularity'>12.3 Granularity is a function of where the file sits</h2>
  <p>
    A thrown error <strong>bubbles up to the nearest ancestor boundary</strong> and no further.
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='critical' /> That single rule decides everything about how a failure degrades your UI. One
    <code>error.tsx</code> at the app root means any throw, anywhere, blanks the entire app to a single
    generic screen. An <code>error.tsx</code> at each meaningful segment means a throw is caught at the
    smallest segment that has one: its layout, its siblings, and every ancestor keep rendering, and only
    the failed segment shows a fallback. <strong
      >How granular your error handling is equals how deep your <code>error.tsx</code> files go.</strong
    > This is the exact parallel to granular Suspense boundaries in <a
      href='11-suspense-streaming.html#streaming'>§11.2</a
    >: more boundaries, smaller blast radius.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">app/ - error.tsx at each level you want to contain a failure</span>app/
├── error.tsx                  <span class="tok-com">last resort for the app's own routes</span>
├── layout.tsx                 <span class="tok-com">app shell (§10.4)</span>
└── programs/
    ├── error.tsx              <span class="tok-com">catches throws in the programs area</span>
    ├── layout.tsx
    └── [id]/
        ├── error.tsx          <span class="tok-com">catches throws for one program</span>
        └── reports/
            ├── error.tsx      <span class="tok-com">catches throws in just the report</span>
            └── page.tsx</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      Each <code>error.tsx</code> catches throws from its own segment down to the next boundary below
      it. A failure in the report is contained to the report; the program header, the sibling tabs, and
      the app shell never find out. Delete the deep ones and the same failure bubbles up until it blanks
      a much larger region.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

