  <h2 id='not-caught'>12.4 What error.tsx will not catch</h2>
  <p>
    The boundary sits <em>inside</em> the segment's layout, wrapping the page. Three consequences follow,
    and each is a common surprise.
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='very' />
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong
        >An error in the segment's own <code>layout.tsx</code> is not caught by that segment's
        <code>error.tsx</code>.</strong
      > The boundary is a child of the layout, so a throw in the layout is above it; it bubbles to the
      <em>parent</em> segment's boundary. If a layout does real work that can fail, it needs a boundary
      one level up.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>An error in the root layout is caught only by <code>global-error.tsx</code>.</strong>
      The root layout is above every <code>error.tsx</code>, so its failure needs the one special
      boundary that replaces the root layout entirely, which is why global error UI must render its
      own <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Errors in event handlers and async callbacks are not render errors</strong>, so no
      render boundary catches them. Handle those where they happen: catch and store in state, or let
      a <code>startTransition</code> throw bubble to the nearest boundary (<a
        href='09-server-resolvable-state.html#url-writes'>§9.4</a
      >).
    </li>
  </ul>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">app/global-error.tsx - the last resort; replaces the root layout</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export default function</span> <span class="tok-fn">GlobalError</span>({ error, reset }: <span class="tok-fn">Props</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    <span class="tok-com">{/* It replaces the root layout, so it renders its own document. */}</span>
    &lt;html&gt;
      &lt;body&gt;
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">RootErrorState</span> <span class="tok-prop">onRetry</span>={reset} /&gt;
      &lt;/body&gt;
    &lt;/html&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      This runs only when the root layout itself failed, which means everything else has already
      failed too. Keep it dead simple and dependency-free: it is the screen of last resort, and it
      is the one boundary that must supply the <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> and <code
        >&lt;body&gt;</code
      >
      the broken root layout would have.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <h2 id='parallel-routes'>12.5 Isolate regions with parallel routes</h2>
  <p>
    Segment boundaries follow the URL, but a single page often shows several independent regions
    that can fail on their own: a summary card, a header, an activity feed, each with its own data.
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='very' /> Give each region its own <strong>parallel-route slot</strong> (<a
      href='14-blocking-requests.html#parallel-routes'>§14.3</a
    >), and each slot, being its own segment, takes its own <code>error.tsx</code> (and its own <code
      >loading.tsx</code
    >). Now a throw in the summary's data renders the summary slot's fallback while the header and
    the feed keep rendering and streaming, untouched. This is the finest isolation the file system
    alone can express: one region's failure contained to that region, decided by the route tree.
  </p>

