  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">app/programs/[id]/@summary/error.tsx - a fallback scoped to one slot</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export default function</span> <span class="tok-fn">SummaryError</span>({ reset }: <span class="tok-fn">Props</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;<span class="tok-fn">SlotError</span> <span class="tok-prop">label</span>=<span class="tok-str">"Summary unavailable"</span> <span class="tok-prop">onRetry</span>={reset} /&gt;;
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      Each parallel slot is a segment, so it gets the full file toolkit: its own
      <code>error.tsx</code>, its own <code>loading.tsx</code>, its own streaming lifecycle. The
      layout renders the slots side by side (<a href='14-blocking-requests.html#parallel-routes'
        >§14.3</a
      >); their failures never reach one another.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <h2 id='custom-boundaries'>12.6 Below the segment: wrap the component yourself</h2>
  <p>
    The file conventions stop at the route segment. When you need finer isolation than the smallest
    segment, a grid of a dozen widgets where any one can fail on its own data, a list where a single
    row must degrade without taking its neighbors, you place the boundary by hand.
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='very' /> Wrap each unit in a custom error boundary (the community-standard
    <code>react-error-boundary</code>, or a small class component) exactly the way you wrap it in
    <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code>: the boundary is chosen by the composer and placed around the
    unit, not baked into it. The boundary is a Client Component, but its children can be server
    components, so the grid itself stays on the server.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">WidgetGrid.tsx - per-widget isolation, below what error.tsx can reach</span><span class="tok-kw">import</span> { ErrorBoundary } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"react-error-boundary"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import</span> { Suspense } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"react"</span>;

<span class="tok-com">/* A server component: it renders a client boundary around a server
   child, which is allowed. No "use client" here. */</span>
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">WidgetGrid</span>({ widgets }: { widgets: <span class="tok-fn">Widget</span>[] }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;div <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"grid grid-cols-3 gap-4"</span>&gt;
      {widgets.map((w) =&gt; (
        &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ErrorBoundary</span> <span class="tok-prop">key</span>={w.id} <span class="tok-prop">FallbackComponent</span>={WidgetError}&gt;
          &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span> <span class="tok-prop">fallback</span>={&lt;<span class="tok-fn">WidgetSkeleton</span> /&gt;}&gt;
            &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Widget</span> <span class="tok-prop">id</span>={w.id} /&gt;
          &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Suspense</span>&gt;
        &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">ErrorBoundary</span>&gt;
      ))}
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      One widget can fail, show its fallback, and be retried without touching the other eleven. Each
      unit owns its loading (<code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code>) and its failure (<code
        >&lt;ErrorBoundary&gt;</code
      >) as a matched pair, the same shape as
      <code>loading.tsx</code> + <code>error.tsx</code>, just below the granularity the file system
      can express. The grid stays a server component; only the boundary is client.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">WidgetError.tsx - the fallback the grid hands each boundary</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">import type</span> { FallbackProps } <span class="tok-kw">from</span> <span class="tok-str">"react-error-boundary"</span>;

<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">WidgetError</span>({ error, resetErrorBoundary }: <span class="tok-fn">FallbackProps</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;div <span class="tok-prop">className</span>=<span class="tok-str">"flex min-h-full items-center justify-center"</span>&gt;
      &lt;button <span class="tok-prop">onClick</span>={resetErrorBoundary}&gt;Couldn't load. Retry&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      The same fill-the-reserved-space discipline as an empty or loading state (<a
        href='10-containers-content-shifting.html'>§10</a
      >): the fallback occupies the widget's cell, so a failure does not collapse the grid or shift
      its neighbors.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <h2 id='no-own-errors'>12.7 Components must not define their own error states</h2>
  <p>
    This is the error twin of <a href='11-suspense-streaming.html#no-own-loading'>§11.3</a>, and it
    is the mistake that makes a codebase impossible to make resilient later.
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='critical' /> A component that wraps its own render in <code>try/catch</code> and returns
    a private "couldn't load" message has <strong
      >swallowed a decision that belongs to whoever composed it</strong
    >. The parent can no longer choose a different fallback for a different context, cannot <code
      >reset()</code
    > or retry it, and, worst of all, the error never reaches an error boundary or your logging, so a
    real bug renders as a shrug and disappears. The boundary is placed <em>around</em> the component,
    by its composer, exactly like Suspense.
  </p>

  <Compare>
    <CompareCol kind='bad' label='✕ The component owns its failure'>
      <pre
        class='code v-bad'
        is:raw><span class="filename">Widget.tsx</span><span class="tok-kw">export async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">Widget</span>({ id }: <span class="tok-fn">Props</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">let</span> data;
  <span class="tok-kw">try</span> {
    data = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">getWidget</span>(id);
  } <span class="tok-kw">catch</span> {
    <span class="tok-com">// swallows the throw; the parent, the
    // boundary, and your logs never see it
    return</span> &lt;div&gt;Couldn't load.&lt;/div&gt;;
  }
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;<span class="tok-fn">WidgetBody</span> <span class="tok-prop">data</span>={data} /&gt;;
}</pre>
    </CompareCol>
    <CompareCol kind='good' label='✓ The composer owns the boundary'>
      <pre
        class='code v-good'
        is:raw><span class="filename">Widget.tsx</span><span class="tok-kw">export async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">Widget</span>({ id }: <span class="tok-fn">Props</span>) {
  <span class="tok-com">// Let it throw. The boundary the PARENT
  // placed (§12.1, §12.6) picks the fallback,
  // can retry it, and reports it.</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> data = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">getWidget</span>(id);
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;<span class="tok-fn">WidgetBody</span> <span class="tok-prop">data</span>={data} /&gt;;
}</pre>
    </CompareCol>
  </Compare>

  <p>
    The trap wears a client disguise too, and it is the more common one. A data hook returns
    <code>{'{'} data, error {'}'}</code>, which practically invites you to branch on
    <code>error</code> in the component and render a private message, burying the failure exactly as the
    server <code>try/catch</code> did: the boundary you placed never fires, and your logging never sees
    it. <strong>Make the hook throw instead</strong>, so the same boundary catches server and client
    failures alike. Most libraries have a switch for it (SWR's <code>suspense</code>, TanStack
    Query's <code>throwOnError</code> or <code>useSuspenseQuery</code>); if yours does not, rethrow
    it yourself.
  </p>

  <Compare>
    <CompareCol kind='bad' label='✕ Branch on the hook' s error'>
      <pre
        class='code v-bad'
        is:raw><span class="filename">ProgramCard.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramCard</span>({ id }: <span class="tok-fn">Props</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { data, error } = <span class="tok-fn">useProgram</span>(id);
  <span class="tok-com">// same swallow, client-side: the
  // boundary and the logs never see it
  if</span> (error) <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;div&gt;Couldn't load.&lt;/div&gt;;
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;<span class="tok-fn">CardBody</span> <span class="tok-prop">data</span>={data} /&gt;;
}</pre>
    </CompareCol>
    <CompareCol kind='good' label='✓ Let the hook throw to the boundary'>
      <pre
        class='code v-good'
        is:raw><span class="filename">ProgramCard.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">export function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramCard</span>({ id }: <span class="tok-fn">Props</span>) {
  <span class="tok-com">// throwOnError (or suspense) sends the
  // failure to the nearest boundary, §12.1</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { data } = <span class="tok-fn">useProgram</span>(id, { throwOnError: <span class="tok-kw">true</span> });
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;<span class="tok-fn">CardBody</span> <span class="tok-prop">data</span>={data} /&gt;;
}</pre>
    </CompareCol>
  </Compare>

  <p>
    The caveat is the same one as loading and empty (<a href='11-suspense-streaming.html#orthogonal'
      >§11.6</a
    >): <strong>an expected condition is not an error.</strong> An empty result, a failed form validation,
    a missing record the user asked for by id, these are normal outcomes you
    <em>return</em> (a value, an empty state, <code>notFound()</code>), not exceptions you throw.
    Boundaries are for the genuinely unexpected: the fetch that fell over, the invariant that broke.
    Throwing for the expected turns a boundary into control flow and buries real failures in the
    noise.
  </p>

  <Callout kind='voice' title='Loading and failing are the same shape'>
    <p>
      Everything in this chapter rhymes with <a href='11-suspense-streaming.html'>§11</a> on purpose.
      A region has two ways to not-yet-show its content: it is still waiting, or it broke. Suspense handles
      the first, an error boundary the second, and in well-built UI they are declared together, around
      the same region, at the same granularity. A widget that has its own
      <code>&lt;Suspense&gt;</code> but no boundary is half-finished; a segment with
      <code>loading.tsx</code> but no <code>error.tsx</code> is a page that streams beautifully right
      up until the moment it blanks.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id='rules'>The rules</h2>
  <table class='tbl'>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Rule</th>
        <th>Scope</th>
        <th>Importance</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Components <strong>never define their own error states</strong>; a boundary placed around
          them does.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Put an <strong><code>error.tsx</code> at every segment</strong> you want a failure contained
          to; depth is the blast radius.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='next' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Remember what a segment's <code>error.tsx</code>
          <strong>cannot catch</strong>: its own layout, the root layout (<code
            >global-error.tsx</code
          >), and event handlers.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='next' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='very' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Isolate independent regions with <strong>parallel-route slots</strong>, each with its own
          <code>error.tsx</code>.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='next' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='very' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          Below the segment, wrap units in a <strong>custom boundary paired with Suspense</strong>;
          the grid stays a server component.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='very' /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <strong>Expected conditions are not errors</strong>: return an empty state or
          <code>notFound()</code>; throw only the unexpected.
        </td>
        <td><Chip k='react' /></td>
        <td><Chip k='critical' /></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <GoDeeper>
    <p>
      <a
        href='https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started/error-handling'
        target='_blank'
        rel='noopener'>Next.js: Error Handling</a
      >
      is the reference for <code>error.tsx</code>, <code>global-error.tsx</code>, and the
      expected-vs-uncaught distinction.
      <a
        href='https://react.dev/reference/react/Component#catching-rendering-errors-with-an-error-boundary'
        target='_blank'
        rel='noopener'>React: error boundaries</a
      >
      explains what render boundaries do and do not catch, and
      <a href='https://github.com/bvaughn/react-error-boundary' target='_blank' rel='noopener'
        >react-error-boundary</a
      > is the component most teams reach for below the segment level.
    </p>
  </GoDeeper>
